TONE CONTROL
================================================================================

The script must hit hard, but it must not sound fake.

Hard-hitting means:
- sharp
- tense
- visual
- serious
- emotionally loaded
- fact-driven
- high-stakes
- cinematic

Hard-hitting does NOT mean:
- shouting
- fake drama
- overusing words like “shocking”
- making unsupported claims
- turning every line into a punchline
- exaggerating facts

Avoid soft lines like:

“The situation was very serious.”
“This was an important issue.”
“People were worried.”
“This created concern.”
“The government took notice.”
“This was a major development.”
“The incident shocked everyone.”
“The issue became important.”

Replace with stronger lines:

“The system was running out of room.”

“The clock was moving faster than the system.”

“One wrong move could turn a mission into a disaster.”

“The enemy was not just hiding. He was calculating.”

“The room did not need speeches. The map said enough.”

“What looked like delay was actually pressure building.”

“This was no longer about one man. It was about whether the system could still strike back.”

“The government could not afford silence anymore.”

“The enemy had not just crossed a line. They had tested the state itself.”

“What looked like delay from the outside was actually a room full of impossible choices.”

“The situation was no longer under control. It was being held together by timing, intelligence, and nerve.”

“One wrong move, and the story would not end as an operation. It would end as a disaster.”

NO SOFT LANGUAGE / HARD IMPACT RULE:
The script must never sound soft, neutral, or emotionally flat.

Every sentence should carry at least one of these:
- pressure
- danger
- consequence
- doubt
- visual weight
- emotional weight
- strategic meaning
- forward movement

If a sentence sounds like a news report, rewrite it as a scene.
If a sentence sounds neutral, add consequence.
If a line sounds soft, make it sharper.
If the story slows down, bring in danger, pressure, or a question.

The narration should feel controlled, but never weak.
It should sound like a serious documentary where every line pushes the story forward.

